  "answer_text": "It looks like **altavista.com** no longer exists as an independent search engine. Here's a summary of what happened to it:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd0d AltaVista \u2014 A Brief History\n\n- AltaVista (meaning \"high view\" in Spanish), based in Palo Alto, California, was a web search engine. Launched in **December 1995**, it was the first \"full text\"/boolean searchable index of the World Wide Web.\n\n- It was the result of a research project started in the summer of 1995 at Digital's Research Laboratories in Palo Alto, California, combining a fast web crawler with scalable indexing software.\n\n- Web traffic increased steadily from 300,000 hits on the first day to more than **80 million hits per day** by 1997, making it one of the most popular websites at the time. However, traffic plummeted shortly thereafter due to competition from **Google Search** and others.\n\n- In 1996, AltaVista became the exclusive provider of search results for Yahoo. In 1997, it launched **Babel Fish**, a web-based machine translation application that translated text or webpages.\n\n- In January 1998, Compaq acquired DEC for $9.6 billion, and as part of the transaction, **Compaq acquired AltaVista**.\n\n- In February 2003, **Overture Services** acquired AltaVista for $140 million. In July 2003, **Yahoo** acquired Overture for $1.63 billion.\n\n- In **June 2013**, the domain name was redirected to **Yahoo Search**.\n\n- AltaVista is also credited with implementing the **first CAPTCHA** to prevent fraudulent account registrations and internet bots from automatically adding pages to its web index.\n\n---\n\nSo if you visit **altavista.com** today, you will simply be redirected to **Yahoo Search**. The iconic pioneer of early web searching is no more as a standalone product.",
